                <pb facs="0285" n="199" sameAs="frus:frus1927v01/pg199"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">500.A15/483: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chief of the American Representation on the Preparatory Commission</hi>
                        (<hi rendition="#italic">Gibson</hi>)</head>
                <opener><seg rendition="#center">[Paraphrase]</seg></opener>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1927-04-11T14:00:00-05:00"><hi rendition="#italic">April 11,
                                1927—2 p.m.</hi></date></dateline></opener>
                <p>114. Your No. 227, April 10, 6 p.m. The Department, as might be expected,
                    disagrees entirely with Boncour’s position that to continue discussion of other
                    phases of draft convention would be futile without an agreement on naval
                    matters. If, however, an adjournment is desired, the Department perceives no
                    objection.</p>
                <p>No conflict with the Three-Power Naval Conference is to be feared, in any event,
                    as the Department has maintained the feasibility of carrying on these
                    conversations during the work of the Preparatory Commission.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Kellogg</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
